<h1>Offline synthetic import-to-export workflow</h1>
<p>easyNIS is experimental. No licensed-data year is analysis ready, and no
annual support, clinical validity or scientific approval is claimed here.
All records, year labels and code assignments in this example are invented.</p>

<h2>Run the installed example</h2>
<p>Install DBI, duckdb and survey, then install easyNIS. This example needs no
network or licensed files. It creates a local temporary parquet file, imports
it and checks its structure. The complete invented population has 120
discharges in four hospitals and two strata.</p>
<p>The code set matches A001 exactly in principal diagnosis slot 1, without
normalization. Missing codes count as no match. The full survey design is
constructed before selecting the matching domain. The design uses hospital
with-replacement variance, unscaled weights, no finite-population correction
and rejection of singleton strata. Unknown domain membership fails.</p>
<!--begin.rcode setup, include=FALSE
knitr::opts_chunk$set(error = FALSE)
end.rcode-->
<!--begin.rcode run-example
library(easyNIS)
outputs <- source(system.file("examples/synthetic-workflow.R", package = "easyNIS"))$value
end.rcode-->
<p>The returned list contains paths named <code>report</code>, <code>csv</code>
and <code>html</code>. In your own session, use <code>browseURL(outputs$html)</code>
to open the HTML table. Copy all three files together to an existing directory
before ending R, so the Markdown report's relative links keep working:</p>
<pre>file.copy(unlist(outputs), "existing-output-directory", overwrite = FALSE)</pre>
<p>The script closes its DuckDB connection and removes its input parquet file,
including on error. An unsuccessful run removes its incomplete output directory.
It preserves caller survey options; incompatible inference options cause an error.</p>

<h2>Read the reviewed table</h2>
<p>The stay row estimates mean LOS in days, excluding missing outcomes within
the domain. Each hospital contributes 12 observed domain discharges, for 48
in total. Independent hospital-level ratio arithmetic in the executable script
checks mean 34/7, standard error approximately 0.3763 and a two-sided 95 percent
Wald t interval with explicitly supplied df 2. These are fixture choices,
not recommended analysis policies.</p>
<p>Disclosure review suppresses raw counts 1 through 10, displays zero and
requires two contributing hospitals. No additive margins are declared. The
rare indicator row is suppressed. The display below reads only the reviewed
CSV presentation; blank cells contain no suppressed numeric results.</p>
<!--begin.rcode reviewed-table, results='asis', echo=TRUE
reviewed <- utils::read.csv(outputs$csv, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
local({
  old <- options(knitr.kable.NA = "")
  on.exit(options(old))
  knitr::kable(reviewed, format = "html", escape = TRUE, digits = 4,
    caption = "Invented reviewed results. Display rounded to four decimal places.")
})
end.rcode-->
<p>CSV preserves shown doubles with 17 significant digits, or whole numbers
in full, and refuses formula-leading text. The separately exported HTML uses
four significant digits for estimates, standard errors and intervals and
includes disclosure notes. Display rounding does not change inference.
The bounded disclosure procedure does not check all larger combinations of
declared margins or undeclared relations. External publication needs human review.</p>

<h2>Keep the declarations with the outputs</h2>
<p>The Markdown report lists R and package versions, code and domain policies,
design fields and methods, weight and estimand meaning, outcome missingness,
variance, df, confidence, row definitions and disclosure policy. It omits raw
records, suppressed results, audit counts, local source paths and retained
design/provenance objects. This fixed report is not a general report generator
or a guarantee that arbitrary caller labels are free of identifying text.</p>
<h2>Pool six invented years and check a model</h2>
<p>The next example constructs full designs for invented labels 2017 through
2022, with reused raw identifiers and explicit year-specific hospital/stratum
keys. In year index i from 1 to 6, weights multiply by i and observed LOS adds
i-1 days. It pools before domain selection, with intent
<code>average_annual_total</code> and native weight divisor 6. The pooled mean
weights supplied discharges; it is not the arithmetic average of annual means.</p>
<!--begin.rcode pooled-example
pooled_outputs <- source(system.file("examples/pooled-model-workflow.R", package = "easyNIS"))$value
end.rcode-->
<p>Independent hospital WR calculations check the mean 172/21, SE approximately
0.1994, average annual LOS total 4816 and its variance 106176. The declared
95 percent t intervals use df 12. An internal Gaussian identity model
<code>LOS ~ 1</code> excludes missing outcomes and verifies its intercept and
sandwich SE against the same independent mean reference. Its coefficient is
on the response scale in days, with no offset, exponentiation or causal claim.
The model and descriptive means each include 288 observed domain discharges
and exclude 72 missing outcomes. These counts are invented fixture facts.</p>
<!--begin.rcode pooled-reviewed-table, results='asis'
pooled_reviewed <- utils::read.csv(pooled_outputs$csv, stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
local({
  old <- options(knitr.kable.NA = "")
  on.exit(options(old))
  knitr::kable(pooled_reviewed, format = "html", escape = TRUE, digits = 4,
    caption = "Invented pooled descriptive results. Display rounded to four decimal places.")
})
end.rcode-->
<p>The same explicit disclosure policy suppresses the rare row. Only reviewed
descriptive values and a fixed declarations/version report are written.
No runtime model coefficient, sample-accounting or retained design/provenance
object is exported in these three files. The fixed invented counts above
describe the fixture. Regression-table disclosure review and export are unsupported.
Use <code>browseURL(pooled_outputs$html)</code> and copy all three returned
files together before ending R, as for the single-year example.</p>
<p>Word has a separate API contract. Annual metadata, conversion provenance,
performance, licensed validation and scientific approval remain separate gates.
Neither walkthrough establishes real-year validity or a supported memory limit.</p>
<!--begin.rcode cleanup, include=FALSE
unlink(dirname(outputs$report), recursive = TRUE)
unlink(dirname(pooled_outputs$report), recursive = TRUE)
end.rcode-->
</body>
